sidgwick-methods-3.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 3 ways: sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2 has "not_designed" and "not_designed → secondary_not_intended"; sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2.2 has "not_designed"; they differ when only "the secret crime was not designed for discovery" is true, which the second allows; sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
sidgwick-methods-3.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2, to: Suppose "the secret crime was not designed for discovery" is true and "the secondary evil caused by the crime was not intended by the criminal" is false. Is S5, "But it remains true that it was not designed for discovery; and therefore that this part of the evil caused by the crime was not intended by the criminal.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2 has "intends_right ∨ no_harm_intended → ¬easy" and "secrecy_not_less_grave ∧ secondary_part ∧ secrecy_cuts → secrecy_difficulty" and "secrecy_difficulty → ¬easy"; sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2.2 has "secrecy_not_less_grave ∧ secondary_part ∧ secrecy_cuts → secrecy_difficulty" and "not_designed → secondary_not_intended" and "intends_right ∨ no_harm_intended ∨ secrecy_difficulty → ¬easy"; they differ when only "it is easy to state clear principles for determining the gravity of crimes, taking the intention only into account", "we do not commonly think that a crime is rendered less grave by being kept perfectly secret", "a great part of the harm done by a crime is the secondary evil of the alarm and insecurity it causes" and "the secret crime was not designed for discovery" are true, which the first allows; sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2.3 has "secrecy_not_less_grave ∧ secondary_part ∧ secrecy_cuts → secrecy_difficulty" and "intends_right ∧ no_harm_intended ∧ secrecy_difficulty ∧ legal_fiction → ¬easy"
sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2 and sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2.2 take for granted "if the secret crime was not designed for discovery, then the secondary evil caused by the crime was not intended by the criminal"; sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2.3 does not
sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2 and sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r2.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the secret crime was not designed for discovery, then the secondary evil caused by the crime was not intended by the criminal"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
